EXT. SEAWALL PROMENADE - TWILIGHT

A curved stone seawall at the edge of a resort town. Gas lamps flicker on. Waves slap the base of the wall. A band plays faintly from a pavilion down the shore.

CECILIA MONTGOMERY-VANE, thirties, stands at the rail in a white evening gown and a traveling cloak. A suitcase sits at her feet. Twenty yards away, WILLIAM HARCOURT, thirties, in a rumpled dinner jacket, runs toward her with his tie undone, waving a train ticket. A porter, SIMEON ABARA, fifties, trails behind him, struggling with a hatbox.

William skids to a halt. Cecilia turns. For a long moment they simply look at each other, both breathless, her cloak lifting in the wind, his tie whipping.

WILLIAM
You're still here.

CECILIA
I missed the six o'clock.

WILLIAM
I know. I heard.

CECILIA
How?

WILLIAM
I asked the stationmaster. He said a woman in white had been sitting on the platform for two hours, refusing to board.

CECILIA
I was thinking.

WILLIAM
About what?

CECILIA
About whether you'd come.

Simeon arrives, wheezing, and sets the hatbox on the stones. He looks from Cecilia to William and then, discreetly, studies the horizon.

SIMEON
Madam, shall I wait?

CECILIA
Please. Just a moment, Simeon.

Cecilia and William step toward each other at once, then stop at the same time, a foot apart. William drops the ticket. A gust takes it. Cecilia lunges, William lunges, their hands collide on the paper, and they both laugh, ruefully, holding it between them.

WILLIAM
It's one-way.

CECILIA
Mine, too.

WILLIAM
Where were you going?

CECILIA
Anywhere you weren't.

WILLIAM
And now?

CECILIA
(softly)
Anywhere you are.

He takes her hand. Simeon coughs politely, picks up the hatbox, and the suitcase, and turns his back with great ceremony.
